WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.550
- What would you say if I told you

00:00:01.550 --> 00:00:04.040
that everything you've been
taught about a healthy diet

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:05.205
has been a lie?

00:00:05.205 --> 00:00:06.650
(woman screaming)

00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:08.070
Well, maybe it's not that bad,

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:10.380
but between nutritional
research, lobbyist groups,

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:11.480
and outdated science,

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:14.520
some really poor eating
choices have been recommended.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:16.110
Let's look at how they've confused us

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:18.060
and examine what we
really should be eating.

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.337
Thanks to Walmart+ for
sponsoring this video.

00:00:20.337 --> 00:00:21.170
Beoo!

00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:23.350
The United States first
implemented dietary guidance

00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:26.460
in 1894, and it changed constantly

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:29.170
because of things like the
Depression, World War rationing,

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:31.410
and the discovery of
vitamins and minerals.

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:34.630
Things didn't get firmly
established 'til 1956

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:36.890
when the USDA announced
the dietary guidance

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:39.230
known as the four basic food groups.

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:41.280
This became the main education tool

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:44.990
for American school children
for the next 36 years.

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:47.020
So what were they pushing people to eat?

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:50.140
If there are four basic food
groups, as they said in 1956,

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:52.360
what do you think those
four basic food groups are?

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:53.350
- Proteins.

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:55.890
- Poultry, vegetables, dessert. (chuckles)

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:58.480
- Protein, vegetables, fruit.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:00.450
- Protein and fruits.

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:01.450
Oh, that's not right.

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:03.807
- Steak, chicken, eggs.

00:01:03.807 --> 00:01:05.610
- How about those fruits
and vegetables, man?

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:07.810
The four basic food
groups were as follows:

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:11.630
milk, meats, fruits and
vegetables, and grains.

00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:14.470
These foods were introduced
as part of a balanced meal,

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:16.920
and the recommendation was
that you eat all of these foods

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:17.880
every single day.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:20.630
However, these recommendations
had major flaws.

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:22.340
For example, there was no distinction made

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:24.570
between refined grains, like white bread,

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:26.170
and whole grains, like whole wheat bread.

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:28.670
And it's an important one to
make because whole wheat bread

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:31.070
takes more energy for your
body to break it down,

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:32.870
therefore it doesn't
spike your blood sugar.

00:01:32.870 --> 00:01:34.210
It doesn't spike your insulin.

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:35.710
These are really important factors

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:37.860
that affect the metabolic
state within your body.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:39.320
The worst part of this guidance

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:41.190
is that it provided no guidance

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:43.270
for the proportions of how
much you should be eating.

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:46.240
In fact, in a single day, you
can have a well-balanced meal

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:48.370
with a gallon of milk, a rib-eye steak,

00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:50.580
peanut butter and jelly
sandwich on white bread,

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:52.130
and a single piece of broccoli.

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:54.210
I don't think that's so healthy.

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:55.300
And kind of weird.

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:57.830
Well, then things then
got better. Sort of.

00:01:57.830 --> 00:01:59.830
Over the years, the USDA had realized

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:02.470
there should be a bit more
nuance in how people eat,

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:04.870
so they introduced the
idea of proportionality.

00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:07.540
A great move, unfortunately,
the USDA often relied

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:09.670
on industry experts to help craft

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:11.520
and approve official dietary guidance.

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:13.770
This influence from groups
like the dairy, corn,

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:16.360
and meat industry led
to dramatic messaging

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:17.560
surrounding the importance of things

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:19.640
like low fat or fat free foods

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:21.640
with zero recognition of the benefits

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:24.010
of things like healthy
fats and clean oils.

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:25.310
But while they suggested eating

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:26.750
different proportions of foods,

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:29.360
they didn't necessarily
recommend the right proportions.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:33.260
Bread, pasta, cereal, oatmeal,
carbs on cards on carbs.

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:36.260
The bottom of this food pyramid
is literally all grains,

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:39.840
and its recommendations
were 6 to 11 servings a day.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:41.910
There's only 10 servings
of oatmeal in here

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:42.990
and look how much it is.

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:44.630
I mean, look, I'm not here to demonize

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:45.730
a single food ingredient

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:47.730
because whole grains could totally be part

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:48.760
of a healthy diet.

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:51.480
The problem here is if you
look at their recommendations

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.470
and proportions, you
could literally be eating

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:56.350
twice as much white bread
as you do vegetables a day,

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:59.620
and it was permissible and
recommended by this food pyramid.

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:00.610
That's poor guidance.

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:04.160
Also, America was going through
its fat free, low fat kick,

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:06.410
which meant that we were demonizing fats.

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:08.310
Not all fats are bad, A,

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:11.080
and B, we also poorly understand fats.

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:14.260
Like, do you know the difference
between whole milk and 1%?

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:15.310
This is 2% milk.

00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:17.040
What does the 2% number represents?

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:20.082
- 2% of how much dairy is in it?

00:03:20.082 --> 00:03:21.241
- 2% lactaid.

00:03:21.241 --> 00:03:22.740
- 2% reduced.

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:23.860
- Fat.
- Fat.

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:25.220
- The percent of fat in there.

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:27.180
- Exactly. So it's the
percent of fat by weight.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:28.060
This is whole milk.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:29.210
What percent is in whole milk?

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:31.000
- Just all the fat. All of it.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:31.930
- 100%.

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:32.763
- Probably zero.

00:03:32.763 --> 00:03:34.870
- You think this has less fat than the 2%?

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:35.978
- Yes.
- 10%.

00:03:35.978 --> 00:03:37.316
- 80%, 80%.

00:03:37.316 --> 00:03:38.825
- 100%.
- 1%.

00:03:38.825 --> 00:03:40.242
- 50%
- 4400.

00:03:42.652 --> 00:03:44.378
- Percent.
- 40%.

00:03:44.378 --> 00:03:45.390
- 3 or 4%.

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:46.710
- You're right. That's really accurate.

00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:47.630
How did you know?

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:48.660
- I'm just guessing.

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:50.130
- And forgetting milk for a second,

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:53.290
once you remove fat food
begins to taste pretty badly.

00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:55.500
So what did we do? Threw
a ton of sugar in there.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:56.333
What did that do?

00:03:56.333 --> 00:03:58.180
Spike our insulin, spike our blood sugar,

00:03:58.180 --> 00:03:59.610
make our waistlines get bigger.

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:02.707
And here we are right in the
middle of an obesity epidemic.

00:04:02.707 --> 00:04:03.540
(image dinging)

